Any correct algorithm is accepted. A good answer names its trade-offs. Here's an in-place **quicksort** (Hoare partition, middle pivot), recursing into the smaller half so stack depth stays O(log n):

```go
// SortInts is an in-place quicksort with a middle pivot (Hoare partition),
// recursing into the smaller half to keep the stack O(log n).
func SortInts(a []int) {
	for len(a) > 1 {
		p := a[len(a)/2]
		i, j := 0, len(a)-1
		for i <= j {
			for a[i] < p {
				i++
			}
			for a[j] > p {
				j--
			}
			if i <= j {
				a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]
				i++
				j--
			}
		}
		if j+1 < len(a)-i {
			SortInts(a[:j+1])
			a = a[i:]
		} else {
			SortInts(a[i:])
			a = a[:j+1]
		}
	}
}
```

Verified with the harness (5000 random `quick.Check` cases):
```text
[1 1 2 3 4 5 5 5 6 9]
All checks passed
```

**Complexity**: O(n log n) average, O(n²) worst case (adversarial pivots), O(log n) stack, not stable.

Alternatives to mention: **merge sort** (O(n log n) guaranteed and stable, but O(n) extra memory), **heapsort** (O(n log n) worst case, in place), **insertion sort** for tiny inputs, and **counting sort** if the value range is small. Go's own `slices.Sort` is pdqsort, which adds a heapsort fallback for guaranteed O(n log n). Gotcha: a first-element pivot makes sorted input O(n²).

